::GEO_U03_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF, which vertex corresponds to B? {
=A. E#B and E are in the second position, so they correspond.
~B. D#D corresponds to A, not B.
~C. F#F corresponds to C, not B.
~D. A#A is in the first triangle and corresponds to D.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle PQR &cong; triangle XYZ, which side corresponds to QR? {
~A. XY#XY corresponds to PQ.
=B. YZ#QR uses the second and third vertices, so it corresponds to YZ.
~C. XZ#XZ corresponds to PR.
~D. PQ#PQ is in the first triangle, not the corresponding side in the second.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle LMN &cong; triangle RST, which angle corresponds to &angle;N? {
~A. &angle;R#R corresponds to L.
~B. &angle;S#S corresponds to M.
=C. &angle;T#N and T are in the third position, so they correspond.
~D. &angle;L#L is a vertex in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which congruence statement correctly matches A to D, B to E, and C to F? {
~A. triangle ABC &cong; triangle DFE#This matches B to F and C to E.
~B. triangle ABC &cong; triangle EFD#This matches A to E, not D.
~C. triangle ABC &cong; triangle FED#This reverses several corresponding vertices.
=D. triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF#The vertices are listed in matching order: A-D, B-E, C-F.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What information is needed before concluding two figures are congruent? {
=A. Evidence that corresponding sizes and shapes match.#Congruence requires same size and shape supported by evidence.
~B. They are drawn near each other.#Location on the page does not prove congruence.
~C. They have the same color.#Color is not a geometric property for congruence.
~D. They look similar at a glance.#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle JKL &cong; triangle MNO, which side corresponds to JK? {
~A. MO#MO connects first and third vertices, matching JL.
=B. MN#J-K matches M-N by order.
~C. NO#NO matches KL.
~D. KL#KL is a side in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle RST &cong; triangle UVW, which angle corresponds to &angle;S? {
~A. &angle;U#U corresponds to R.
~B. &angle;W#W corresponds to T.
=C. &angle;V#S and V are both second in the statement.
~D. &angle;T#T corresponds to W, not S.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which side corresponds to AC if triangle ABC &cong; triangle FDE? {
~A. FD#FD matches AB.
~B. DE#DE matches BC.
~C. AB#AB is in the first triangle.
=D. FE#A-C matches F-E because A corresponds to F and C corresponds to E.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Why does the order of letters in a congruence statement matter? {
=A. It identifies corresponding vertices and parts.#The order tells which vertices, sides, and angles match.
~B. It changes the size of the triangles.#Statement order does not change actual size.
~C. It determines the color of the diagram.#Color is not part of geometric correspondence.
~D. It replaces the need for evidence.#Evidence is still needed to establish congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF, which statement follows by corresponding parts? {
~A. &angle;A &cong; &angle;E#E corresponds to B, not A.
=B. &angle;A &cong; &angle;D#A corresponds to D, so those angles are congruent.
~C. AB &cong; EF#AB corresponds to DE, not EF.
~D. BC &cong; DE#BC corresponds to EF, not DE.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If two triangles are congruent, what must be true? {
~A. Only their areas are equal.#Equal area alone does not guarantee congruence.
~B. Only their angles are equal.#Equal angles alone can indicate similarity, not necessarily congruence.
=C. Corresponding sides and angles are congruent.#Congruent triangles have all corresponding sides and angles congruent.
~D. They must be in the same position.#Congruent figures can be moved by rigid transformations.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram marks AB &cong; DE and BC &cong; EF. Which side pair would complete corresponding side evidence for triangle ABC and triangle DEF? {
~A. AC &cong; EF#EF already corresponds to BC.
~B. AB &cong; DF#AB corresponds to DE.
~C. BC &cong; DE#BC corresponds to EF.
=D. AC &cong; DF#A-C corresponds to D-F by the vertex order.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which notation correctly states corresponding angles from triangle CAT &cong; triangle DOG? {
=A. &angle;A &cong; &angle;O#A and O are both second in the congruence statement.
~B. &angle;C &cong; &angle;O#C corresponds to D.
~C. &angle;T &cong; &angle;D#T corresponds to G.
~D. &angle;A &cong; &angle;G#A corresponds to O, not G.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What does CPCTC mean in a congruence context? {
~A. Congruent parts create triangle classification.#This phrase does not state the CPCTC relationship.
=B. Corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent.#CPCTC describes matching sides and angles after triangle congruence is known.
~C. Corresponding points change triangle coordinates.#Coordinates are not the meaning of CPCTC.
~D. Congruent triangles are always equilateral.#Congruent triangles do not have to be equilateral.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle HIK &cong; triangle PQR, which side corresponds to IK? {
~A. PQ#PQ corresponds to HI.
~B. PR#PR corresponds to HK.
=C. QR#I-K corresponds to Q-R by order.
~D. HI#HI is in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement relies only on appearance and should be avoided? {
~A. The triangles are congruent because all corresponding sides are marked congruent.#Corresponding side markings are valid evidence.
~B. The triangles are congruent because a rigid motion maps one onto the other.#Rigid-motion mapping is valid congruence evidence.
~C. The triangles are congruent because matching sides and angles are given.#Given matching parts can support congruence.
=D. The triangles look the same, so they are congruent.#Appearance alone is not enough for congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC &cong; triangle QRS, which side corresponds to CA? {
=A. SQ#C-A corresponds to S-Q by order.
~B. RQ#RQ corresponds to BA.
~C. RS#RS corresponds to BC.
~D. AB#AB is in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which vertex correspondence is shown by triangle WXY &cong; triangle JKL? {
~A. W &leftrightarrow; K#W corresponds to J.
=B. X &leftrightarrow; K#X and K are both second.
~C. Y &leftrightarrow; J#Y corresponds to L.
~D. X &leftrightarrow; L#X corresponds to K, not L.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If two figures are congruent after a translation, what does the translation preserve? {
~A. Only color#Color is not a geometric property.
~B. Only orientation#Translations preserve orientation but also preserve more than orientation.
=C. Distance and angle measure#Translations are rigid transformations, so they preserve distances and angles.
~D. Only area with no side lengths#Congruence requires side lengths and angle measures, not area alone.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which side pair corresponds if triangle BCA &cong; triangle EFD? {
~A. BC &cong; FD#BC corresponds to EF.
~B. BA &cong; EF#BA corresponds to ED.
~C. CA &cong; EF#CA corresponds to FD, not EF.
=D. CA &cong; FD#C-A corresponds to F-D by order.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>What is the best first step when reading a congruence statement? {
=A. Match vertices by their order in the statement.#Vertex order determines correspondence.
~B. Assume the drawings are to scale.#Drawings are not assumed to scale.
~C. Choose the longest side first.#Longest side is not always identified or relevant first.
~D. Ignore vertex order.#Ignoring order causes correspondence errors.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle MNP &cong; triangle ZYX, which angle corresponds to &angle;M? {
~A. &angle;Y#Y corresponds to N.
=B. &angle;Z#M and Z are both first in the statement.
~C. &angle;X#X corresponds to P.
~D. &angle;P#P corresponds to X.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which side corresponds to NP in triangle MNP &cong; triangle ZYX? {
~A. ZY#ZY corresponds to MN.
~B. ZX#ZX corresponds to MP.
=C. YX#N-P corresponds to Y-X.
~D. MN#MN is in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A student says triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF, so AC &cong; EF. What is the mistake? {
~A. AC corresponds to DE, not EF.#DE matches AB.
~B. AC has no corresponding side.#Every side has a corresponding side in congruent triangles.
~C. EF is in the first triangle.#EF is in the second triangle.
=D. AC corresponds to DF, not EF.#A-C matches D-F by statement order.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence can support congruence without relying on appearance? {
=A. A rigid transformation maps one figure exactly onto the other.#Rigid-motion mapping is valid congruence evidence.
~B. The figures are close together.#Location does not prove congruence.
~C. The figures have similar names.#Names alone do not prove size and shape.
~D. The figures are both drawn in black.#Color is not geometric evidence.
}

